<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  modern34 — targetSdk 34, scoped storage.

  Reaches the same OEM recordings through MediaStore plus All-files access
  instead of raw paths. Better on every axis except the one S1 measured, which
  is why both flavours ship and M0 decides.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- UC-14: the OEM recordings folder is not a media collection this app
         owns, and under scoped storage nothing narrower reaches it. This is the
         single most alarming screen in the whole install (R17) and it is the
         price of the preferred capture route on a modern target. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE" />

    <!-- UC-14: the MediaStore audio query that locates the recording, on the
         API 33+ split of READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_AUDIO" />

    <!--
      The `phoneCall` foreground-service type IS used now, and it is declared
      in src/main/AndroidManifest.xml for both flavours.

      The rule this comment used to state is still true: on API 34+ the type
      requires the app to be the default dialer, to hold MANAGE_OWN_CALLS, or to
      be a device owner, and starting an FGS of a type you do not qualify for
      throws SecurityException. What changed is the app — it now declares
      MANAGE_OWN_CALLS, a normal permission with no dialog, which is the route
      CallSentry took on the same handsets.

      It is still not assumed: `CaptureService` tries `phoneCall|microphone`,
      falls back to `microphone|dataSync`, then to no type at all, and records
      which one the OS accepted so the panel can show it per handset. That is a
      measurement on the fleet rather than an argument about the documentation.
      -->

</manifest>
